  "notes": "The LA trio that invented 2000s teen pop-rock, modelled on the mid-tempo band: 80–100 straight with the backbeat on 2&4. The 150–175 punk-leaning band is the other half of their catalogue and is named here rather than encoded. Harmony starts at the instrument: Graham Edwards works from acoustic guitar \"basic chords\", \"so voicings are open-position with ringing strings\" — hence the open voicing, the four guitar-first majors in `keys`, and I–V–vi–IV / vi–IV–I–V with one key per song. Scott Spock handles drum programming and keyboards, and the entry states the crucial qualifier: **live-kit overdubs by session drummers replace programmed parts on the rock songs**, which is why the humanize here is looser than on the Avril artist model that shares the flagship single, and why the perc block carries a snap alongside the tambourine and rim. Three deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and the '&' of 3\", \"snare 2&4 with a rimshot ghost\" (0.28 across \"4e\" and \"4a\"), and \"8th hats, tambourine on the chorus backbeat\". Edwards plays bass as well as guitar — \"root 8ths doubling the guitar, no fills\" — so the bass locks to the kick. The writing method is the entry's real content: the three write only together, treat production as part of songwriting, \"record every idea at full quality to preserve accidents\", and build the artist's lyric and attitude in from the start. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability and `bpm.mode` 90; the roles, rooms, DAW, gear and a flagship song's parameters are sourced, the values are not.",
